## Dead Ends
A hash check alone has a check-to-rename race between cooperating writers. Locking the plan inode is also insufficient because atomic rename replaces that inode. A nonblocking lock on the containing directory avoids both gaps without persistent lock-file cleanup.

## Hints for Future Agents
Directory locking serializes writes in one repository plan folder and can return a busy error even for different plans. External editors and older binaries do not cooperate and can still race the final check. Hashes cover all bytes and accept either hexadecimal case. A user must reread and reconcile after conflict rather than automatically recompute a hash and retry. The writer now accepts a loaded plan with its source bytes instead of only a path. Never mutate source bytes in place. The syscall lock targets the existing macOS/Linux support. Disable hooks for local-only commits.

## Codebase Discoveries
The old commands loaded metadata through loadCorpus and then reread file bytes, allowing an update to mix two versions. Reusing source bytes removes those redundant reads and makes metadata checks and writes consistent. The existing atomic writer cleans up its temporary file on conflict and other failures.

